<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Moghulistan</h1><p class="st-meta">1347 CE – 1462 CE (approx.) · capital Almaliq</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern Chagatai khanate of the Tian Shan and Ili valley, where the Mongol nomads converted to Islam and resisted Timur.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> split in the 1340s, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlat</a> amir Puladchi enthroned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlugh-timur">Tughlugh Timur</a>, a claimed descendant of Chagatai, as khan of the eastern part in 1347. The western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> called this nomadic region and its people Moghulistan, 'land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>'.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlugh-timur">Tughlugh Timur</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> with many of his followers around 1354, and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> in 1360 and 1361. The khanate was nomadic, with powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim</a> cities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-shule">Kashgar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> campaigned against it repeatedly but never conquered it. Khans such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan</a>, grandfather of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, maintained links with Timurid Transoxiana.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Divided by civil war, Moghulistan split in the 1460s between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan</a> in the west and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esen-buqa-ii">Esen Buqa</a>'s line in the east. Yunus later reunited it, but after his death in 1487 his sons divided it again, and the eastern part under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-alaq">Ahmad Alaq</a> became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turpan-khanate">Turpan Khanate</a>. Its western pastures were taken by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people">Kyrgyz</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/moghulistan.jpg" alt="Moghulistan" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chagatai_Khanate_(1490).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1347 CE – 1462 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Almaliq</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tughlugh Timur, Esen Buqa II, Yunus Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chagatai Turkic, Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, <a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eastern Chagatai Khanate, Moghul Khanate, Mogholistan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1404&amp;focus=moghulistan" aria-label="Moghulistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6608 612 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Moghulistan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6928 868L6937 875M6928 868L6921 1054M6928 868L7154 908"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6937 875h0M6921 1054h0M7154 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6928 868h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6928 868h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Almaliq. <a href="/atlas?year=1404&amp;focus=moghulistan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
